-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "A dim, narrow gorge split apart by tectonic forces, al-Siq winds approximately 1.2 kilometres between walls up to 182 metres high to Petra''s most elaborate ruin, Al-Khazneh."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "dim, narrow gorge [S2]; split apart by tectonic forces [S3]; winds approximately 1.2 kilometres [S2]; walls up to 182 metres high [S4]; ends at Petra''s most elaborate ruin, Al-Khazneh [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-012 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "al-Siq is a natural gorge, the main entrance to the ancient Nabatean city of Petra in Jordan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siq]; al-Siq is a dim, narrow gorge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siq]; The Siq was split apart by tectonic forces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siq]; The Siq winds approximately 1.2 kilometres [SUPPORTED: https://pdtra.gov.jo/EN/ListDetails/Historical_Location/1234/1]; The walls of the Siq are up to 182 metres high (sources conflict: Wikipedia gives 91-182 m citing Bourbon 1999; Lonely Planet says 200 m; the Petra National Trust says cliffs up to 100 m; the Petra regional authority says more than 80 m in places; no page settles it) [UNVERIFIABLE, no proving quote: None]; The Siq leads to Petra''s Treasury, Al-Khazneh [SUPPORTED: https://www.visitpetra.jo/en/Location/25]; Al-Khazneh is Petra''s most elaborate ruin [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siq]"}]'::jsonb),
