"lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-013 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Xpuhil is a Maya archaeological site in the Mexican state of Campeche (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xpuhil]; Xpuhil was abandoned for centuries before it was rediscovered [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xpuhil]; Karl Ruppert and John Denison rediscovered Xpuhil [SUPPORTED: https://www.themayanruinswebsite.com/xpuhil.html]; The rediscovery of Xpuhil was in 1938 [SUPPORTED: https://www.themayanruinswebsite.com/xpuhil.html]; Xpuhil is a place of false temples (faux temple towers with non-functioning stairways and doors) [SUPPORTED: https://www.themayanruinswebsite.com/xpuhil.html]; Xpuhil was occupied somewhere between 300 and 1200 AD (the INAH estimate as reported by Wikipedia; note INAH''s own site page gives the fuller chronology 400 BC to 1200 AD, so the 1200 AD end is confirmed and 300 AD is an estimate, not a hard start) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xpuhil]; The occupation of Xpuhil ran until about 1200 AD [SUPPORTED: https://inah.gob.mx/zonas/71-zona-arqueologica-xpuhil]"}]'::jsonb),
